steps.prompt:
Audit every captured catalog record from `catalog-evidence` and classify structural risk without assuming every unusual record is wrong. Stay read-only against Fluid. Read `artifact_dir` from the catalog-evidence STEP_OUTPUT; work from the saved artifacts — do NOT re-walk the live catalog.

Artifact contract (applies to this step): all run artifacts live under the run's artifact directory; every count, list, percentage, and hash you report must be printed by a verification script executed with `run_cli` as `npm exec --yes -- node <script>` — model-recalled or hand-counted numbers are a defect. Scripts must be created with `write_file`, read only saved artifact files, and follow this skeleton: (a) write EXACTLY one JSON object to a summary file (e.g. `<artifact_dir>/verify-summary.json`) and read it back with `read_file` — never parse run_cli's merged stdout/stderr stream; the object is `{"ok":true,...}` with exit 0, or `{"ok":false,"error":{code,message}}` with `process.exit(1)`, and consumers gate on `ok`, never on the mere presence of numbers; (b) refuse to report on data that is not there: assert a minimum expected row count and cross-check any declared count in the input against rows actually parsed — zero defects on zero or partial rows is an ERROR, never a pass; (c) cap every id list at 20 and the field inventory at 200, each with `total` and `truncated` flags, full lists to sibling artifact files; (d) emit an `integrity` block (input file sha256, byte size, declared vs observed row counts) so later steps can prove they reasoned over the same snapshot; (e) be idempotent — reruns must produce byte-identical summaries. If `npm exec` is unavailable, report that as a data gap and stop the affected check — never fall back to hand-counting. Permitted tools for audit work: `read_file`, `write_file`, `list_dir`, `search_files`, `run_cli` (npm/git only), `fluid_catalog_index`, documented v202604 GETs via `fluid_api`, and `query_docs`; do not probe other tools or undocumented paths.

Create `<artifact_dir>/clg-audit.mjs` with `write_file` (or extend the existing verify script) so every inventory below is computed programmatically over `<artifact_dir>/pages/*.json` and `aggregates.json`. The script counts; you classify and judge. Use bounded documented v202604 detail GETs for at most 10 records where the captured data is genuinely ambiguous, saving each response to `<artifact_dir>/details/<id>.json`. Inventories:
- exact normalized-title groups and near-title candidates, retaining every id, lifecycle, price/currency, SKU, variant count, and distinguishing field;
- duplicate non-empty SKU assignments;
- active/public/sellable records with missing or zero price, missing currency, no purchasable variant, missing primary image, blank title, or unusable canonical route;
- price divergence inside an exact-title group, separated by currency, subscription vs one-time price, interval, variant/options, and package quantity when those fields exist;
- option axes and variant combinations, flagging only explicit duplicate combinations or missing required values; never synthesize a Cartesian product;
- bundle markers/components and subscription-plan relationships when the captured response exposes them; otherwise report the audit dimension unavailable;
- optional `catalog_expectations`: minimum counts and required phrases apply only when supplied. Search required phrases only in documented product copy fields, case-insensitively, and report field coverage.

Classify each script-surfaced finding:
- `confirmed_blocker_candidate`: mechanically unlaunchable evidence such as a sellable record with no usable price/currency or purchasable variant, a duplicate non-empty SKU on simultaneously sellable records, or an unusable canonical route;
- `merchant_review`: exact/near duplicate titles, divergent prices, zero prices, missing images, bundle/plan absence, or required-text misses that may be deliberate;
- `informational`: lifecycle and coverage facts with no failure claim.

A duplicate title, zero price, missing bundle marker, or price difference is NEVER automatically a confirmed blocker. Preserve distinct product ids; do not merge, delete, archive, reprice, or publish anything. Never assign avoided-revenue dollars without order/conversion evidence.

Write the complete classified findings to `<artifact_dir>/findings.json`. End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `artifact_dir`, `findings_path`, `audited_ids`, `coverage_percent`, `confirmed_blocker_candidates`, `merchant_review`, `informational`, `dimension_coverage`, `expectation_results`, `currency_blocks`, `data_gaps`, and `fluid_writes_performed: false`. Cap every list at 20 ids; totals must equal the length of the corresponding list in `findings.json`.

Publish a CEO-ready Catalog Launch Guard dashboard and a page-ready More-for-Less brief from the verified flag review.

Read `artifact_dir` from prior STEP_OUTPUTs, then load `aggregates.json`, `findings.json`, and `approval-queue.json` with `read_file` — every number on the dashboard must be copied verbatim from those artifacts or the flag-review STEP_OUTPUT, never recomputed in your head. Preserve every needs-review finding and data gap. Call `list_dashboards`, then call `show_dashboard` with one descriptor:
- title `Catalog Launch Guard`; subtitle names the observation timestamp and says `Read-only audit`; `dataAsOf` equals evidence freshness;
- headline `stat_tiles`: rows audited, coverage percent, confirmed blockers, merchant decisions, and false-positive rate;
- a lifecycle breakdown `stat_rows` (active / draft / archived, sellable vs not) sourced from `aggregates.json`, so total rows are never mistaken for live products;
- a prominent GREEN/AMBER/RED `insight_banner` containing the exact gate reason;
- a risk breakdown `donut_chart` or `stat_rows` for blocker candidates, merchant review, cleared false positives, and unverifiable flags;
- a `mini_table` of confirmed blockers and highest-priority merchant decisions with product id, finding, evidence, and recommended approval action;
- a dimension-coverage card for lifecycle, price/currency, variants/options, SKU, image, route, bundle, subscription plan, and configured expectations, marking unavailable rather than silently dropping a dimension;
- an assumptions/data-quality banner saying anomalies are not automatically errors, no repairs were made, and non-identity fields are as-of the step-1 `observed_at` timestamp.

Use raw numeric metrics with `number`, `compact`, `percent`, or `ratio_percent`; do not pre-format numeric strings or invent revenue. If dashboard validation fails, repair only its descriptor.

Then write a `## CEO value page brief` under 350 words to `<artifact_dir>/brief.md` with `write_file`, containing exactly: `Headline`, `What Mist checked`, `Launch decision`, `What needs approval`, `More for less`, `Controls`, and `Evidence limits`. The headline must use verified coverage and gate counts, not avoided-revenue dollars. In `More for less`, write `Wall-clock run window: reported at final gate.` — the final step fills the measured value; do not invent a runtime or speed multiplier. State that no product, variant, price, bundle, plan, image, or lifecycle record changed.

Saving/showing the local dashboard and writing run artifacts are the only state changes. End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `artifact_dir`, `dashboard_title`, `dashboard_id` when returned, `data_as_of`, `gate`, `coverage`, `confirmed_blockers`, `merchant_decisions`, `false_positive_rate`, `brief_path`, `data_gaps`, and `fluid_writes_performed: false`. Do NOT inline the brief text in STEP_OUTPUT — pass `brief_path` only (step outputs are truncated at 12,000 characters and a truncated brief silently corrupts downstream steps).

Perform the final independent launch-gate review. Read-only against Fluid; do not redo the whole audit and do not repair records.

Artifact contract (applies to this step): all run artifacts live under the run's artifact directory; every count, list, percentage, and hash you report must be printed by a verification script executed with `run_cli` as `npm exec --yes -- node <script>` — model-recalled or hand-counted numbers are a defect. Scripts must be created with `write_file`, read only saved artifact files, and follow this skeleton: (a) write EXACTLY one JSON object to a summary file (e.g. `<artifact_dir>/verify-summary.json`) and read it back with `read_file` — never parse run_cli's merged stdout/stderr stream; the object is `{"ok":true,...}` with exit 0, or `{"ok":false,"error":{code,message}}` with `process.exit(1)`, and consumers gate on `ok`, never on the mere presence of numbers; (b) refuse to report on data that is not there: assert a minimum expected row count and cross-check any declared count in the input against rows actually parsed — zero defects on zero or partial rows is an ERROR, never a pass; (c) cap every id list at 20 and the field inventory at 200, each with `total` and `truncated` flags, full lists to sibling artifact files; (d) emit an `integrity` block (input file sha256, byte size, declared vs observed row counts) so later steps can prove they reasoned over the same snapshot; (e) be idempotent — reruns must produce byte-identical summaries. If `npm exec` is unavailable, report that as a data gap and stop the affected check — never fall back to hand-counting. Permitted tools for audit work: `read_file`, `write_file`, `list_dir`, `search_files`, `run_cli` (npm/git only), `fluid_catalog_index`, documented v202604 GETs via `fluid_api`, and `query_docs`; do not probe other tools or undocumented paths.

Read all completed outputs and QA findings, and `artifact_dir` from prior STEP_OUTPUTs. Then:
1. Re-run the run's committed verification scripts (`clg-verify.mjs`, `clg-audit.mjs`) with `run_cli` and require exit code 0 and printed tallies that match every number in the dashboard, `brief.md`, and prior STEP_OUTPUTs exactly.
2. Call `fluid_catalog_index` with `output_path: "<artifact_dir>/index-verify.json"` and `verify_only: true`. A changed SHA-256 or id-set delta versus step 1 is a FRESHNESS finding, not an automatic failure: report the exact delta, set the gate to AMBER with reason `catalog changed during audit`, and keep RED only when a previously confirmed blocker's evidence no longer reproduces or a new mechanical blocker is proven live.
3. Use bounded documented Product GETs to reproduce every confirmed blocker and a deterministic sample of at least five merchant decisions (or all when fewer).
4. Call `list_dashboards` to confirm the saved artifact.
5. Compute BY SCRIPT the wall-clock run window as the difference between step 1's `observed_at` and the current UTC time, and label it exactly `wall-clock run window (includes QA and review time)` — never present it as pure compute time. Write `<artifact_dir>/brief-final.md` as a copy of `brief.md` with the `More for less` runtime line filled with that labeled value.

Fail when catalog/index reconciliation is incomplete for reasons other than live catalog drift, audit coverage is below 100% of the captured set, a blocker lacks live evidence, a merchant-review anomaly was promoted to an error without proof, currencies/price types were mixed, unavailable fields were hidden, the false-positive calculation/gate is wrong, a Fluid mutation occurred, or the dashboard/brief misstates the evidence.

Return a presenter handoff:
- `Decision`: GREEN, AMBER, or RED plus one sentence;
- `Live proof`: observed timestamp, indexed rows, unique ids, coverage, the per-file SHA-256 list, and the wall-clock run window;
- `Confirmed blockers`: count and ids;
- `Needs merchant decision`: count and top items;
- `Approval boundary`: future write types, with no action taken;
- `Demo path`: dashboard gate -> one evidence-backed flag -> approval queue;
- `CEO page copy`: the path to `brief-final.md` (do not inline it);
- `Unresolved`: all data/QA gaps.

End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `ready: true|false`, `gate`, `dashboard_id`, `live_proof`, `confirmed_blockers`, `merchant_decisions`, `false_positive_rate`, `wall_clock_run_window`, `approval_boundary`, `demo_path`, `brief_final_path`, `fluid_writes_performed: false`, and `unresolved`.
